# steamclient64.guestpe -- declarative source list for the guest x86-64
# steamclient64.dll.
#
# WHY THIS DIRECTORY IS NAMED steamclient64, NOT lsteamclient (Proton's own
# name for it).  A guest LoadLibraryA/W walks find_builtin_dll() in
# dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c, whose prepend_build_dir_path() constructs
# <build_dir>/dlls/<name-without-extension>/<arch_dir>/<name>.<ext> --
# literally the source directory name, not any spec/def LIBRARY line or
# module variable.  Proton gets away with a lsteamclient/ directory because
# its own build stages the result into a Steam-named file afterward; this
# port does not stage anything into a prefix, so the source directory has
# to already be spelled the way the game's LoadLibraryA("steamclient64.dll")
# spells it, or the load fails before this file's contents matter at all.
#
# Every other guest-facing module in this tree is a native ppc64
# implementation plus a spec2thunk trap-stub thunk (see dlls/combase/
# combase.thunks for that shape).  This module has no native half at all --
# see steamclient_guest.h for why a Steamworks vtable can't be served that
# way -- so its "SOURCES" entry is not a normal .c list either: it names
# tools/guestpe/guestpe, which compiles and links these sources with clang's
# x86_64-windows-gnu target into one real PE DLL.  makedep.c's
# output_source_guestpe() is the only code that reads this file; see its
# header comment for the make-dependency side of this contract.
#
# Eight keywords, one per line, "KEYWORD value".  This file uses six of
# them; the other two -- SRCDIR and GUEST-MACHINE -- exist for the 32-bit
# sibling, dlls/steamclient, which compiles THESE sources for i386 out of one
# copy.  See that file for what they mean and why it is a separate module.  A line whose first
# non-blank character is '#' is a comment, and blank lines are ignored --
# this file is never scanned for C-style #include/#pragma the way an
# ordinary source is (the .guestpe extension is absent from makedep.c's
# parse_functions[] table, exactly like .thunks and .spec), so nothing
# above this line has any effect on the build.
#
#   DLL      the output DLL's own name -- documentary only.  The actual
#            target name comes from THIS file's own basename, the same way
#            combase.thunks names combase's thunk DLL.
#   ENTRY    the PE entry point symbol passed to the linker.
#   DEF      module-relative path to the .def export list.
#   IMPORT   the base name of a guest x86-64 thunk DLL (built by ANOTHER
#            module's own "SOURCES = *.thunks" line) this module links
#            against.  makedep resolves it to a real build path with the
#            same submake lookup add_import_libs() uses for ordinary
#            IMPORTS=, and adds it as a REAL Makefile prerequisite: relink
#            when kernel32's export table changes underneath this one.
#   SOURCE   one compilable C source, module-relative.  Each SOURCE line
#            becomes a real Makefile prerequisite of steamclient64.dll, so
#            touching it -- steamrpc.c, say -- rebuilds the DLL.  This is
#            the "add a source, add a line, no makedep change" contract.
#   SRCDIR   module-relative directory that SOURCE and GLOB resolve
#            against, and that supplies the compiler's -I paths.  Unused
#            here: this module's sources are its own.
#   GUEST-MACHINE
#            the guest machine to build for, spelled as the .thunks files
#            spell it.  Absent -- as here -- a .guestpe file builds for
#            x86_64 only; see guestpe_machine_default in tools/makedep.c.
#   GLOB     a shell glob of sources, module-relative, expanded by
#            tools/guestpe/guestpe AT BUILD TIME rather than by makedep.
#            NOT individually tracked: make cannot depend on a filename it
#            doesn't have yet, and hand-listing 39 near-identical
#            winISteam*.c wrappers just to get per-file tracking would cost
#            more in review noise than it buys.  Consequence, stated
#            plainly: adding, removing, or renaming a file that matches a
#            GLOB is not picked up by an incremental "make" -- touch this
#            file (or rerun config.status) afterwards.  Editing the
#            CONTENTS of a file the glob already matched is unaffected --
#            make has no idea the file exists, but the driver recompiles
#            everything the glob matches on every run regardless, so the
#            new contents are in the next build no matter what triggered it.

DLL      steamclient64.dll
ENTRY    DllMain
DEF      steamclient64.def

IMPORT   kernel32
IMPORT   msvcrt
IMPORT   ntdll
IMPORT   user32
IMPORT   ws2_32
# steamclient_main.c writes HKCU\Software\Valve\Steam\ActiveProcess\pid at
# load time with RegCreateKeyExW/RegSetValueExW/RegCloseKey.
IMPORT   advapi32

# Proton's own PE-side entry points and interface-independent glue.
SOURCE   proton/steamclient_main.c
SOURCE   proton/steamclient_generated.c
SOURCE   proton/steam_input_manual.c
SOURCE   proton/steam_matchmaking_manual.c
SOURCE   proton/steam_networking_manual.c

# This port's half of the bridge to the native Steam client -- see
# steamrpc.c's own header for the design, and steamrpc_chkstk.c's for why
# the stack probe clang emits for load_steamclient()'s two 4096-byte
# buffers needs a hand-written implementation here.
SOURCE   steamrpc.c
SOURCE   steamrpc_generated.c
SOURCE   steamrpc_chkstk.c

# The 39 generated per-interface wrappers, one per ISteamXxxNNN version --
# see the GLOB keyword's comment above for why these are not enumerated
# individually, and what that costs.
GLOB     proton/winISteam*.c
